# tests/T28.bd - T28: THE TIER SCALING CARD.
# The reference card for the whole CPU-load programme. Run twice now to lay
# the baseline down; run twice again, COMPLETELY UNMODIFIED, the first thing
# after any CPU upgrade is ever bought.
#
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# ONE QUESTION
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
#   Is get_cpu() a tier-NORMALISED percentage or an absolute tick count, and
#   is the overload kill 5 WALL-CLOCK SECONDS or a FIXED NUMBER OF TICKS -
#   i.e. what exactly does buying a faster CPU change?
#
#   Those are two questions with one instrument between them, which is why
#   they share a card. Both are answered by running the SAME script at two
#   tiers and diffing three numbers. Neither is answerable any other way.
#
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# WHY THIS EXISTS - what is actually at stake
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
#   1. NOBODY KNOWS WHAT TIER THIS MACHINE IS. Every number in field_notes.txt
#      silently assumes base tier, 10 ticks/sec. No trace in the entire corpus
#      records the real rate. menu.bd logs one (nu.local_tick_rate()) but that
#      function reads ~/.config/blackdoor.conf on an unverified Discord claim
#      and FALLS BACK TO 10 when the file is unreadable - so its output may be
#      a default wearing the costume of a measurement. This script measures the
#      rate from its own execution instead, and prints the conf file beside it
#      so the two can be checked against each other for the first time.
#   2. field_notes CONTRADICTS ITSELF ABOUT TIER SCALING. The CPU LOAD section
#      asserts the meter is tier-normalised ("the same code costs
#      proportionally fewer percentage-points ... on a machine with more
#      ticks/sec ... there's nothing to wire in, the adaptation already happens
#      automatically"), and utils/net.bd repeats that as settled guidance. The
#      REFRAMING block 600 lines later reports a "max_cpu_usage_limit that
#      stays equally high regardless of hardware tier". Both cannot be true.
#      Neither has ever been measured. If the second one is right, the first is
#      load-bearing bad advice sitting in two files.
#   3. IF THE KILL IS A TICK COUNT, A CPU UPGRADE BUYS ZERO BURST HEADROOM.
#      The documented rule is "100% for 5 straight seconds". At base tier that
#      is ~50 tick-slots - and both known lethal bursts land on that number
#      (50 unpaced arithmetic lines; ~13 log() iterations at 3-4 lines each =
#      39-52 ticks). The two readings are numerically indistinguishable at base
#      tier and diverge by 8x at Mk.IV. A $500 purchase decision rides on which
#      it is, and so does whether "pace every 5-8 lines" is a tier-independent
#      rule or one that relaxes with hardware.
#   4. IF THE METER IS ABSOLUTE, EVERY PERCENTAGE IN field_notes IS TIER-LOCAL.
#      Every throttle threshold (50 / 80), every "steady state 100", every
#      decay curve would have to be re-read as a statement about one tier only.
#
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# THE TWO HALVES
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
#   PHASE 1 - THE METER'S SCALE. Two paced probe loops, identical in every
#   respect except that arm B executes exactly 3 more lines per second than
#   arm A. That gives two independent tier readouts from one phase:
#       INTERCEPT  floorA - the idle floor. test_cpu_decay measured exactly
#                  10.0 solo and held it 16+ seconds. Tier-normalised meter ->
#                  this must DROP with the tier. Absolute counter -> unchanged.
#       SLOPE      floorB - floorA = what 3 extra executed lines per second are
#                  worth in meter points. This is the better of the two: it is
#                  immune to any constant ambient term hiding inside the floor.
#                  If the floor is (unit * lines) + ambient, only the slope
#                  measures the unit. Tier-normalised -> slope halves on Mk.II.
#   These two can disagree, and that disagreement is itself a result - see
#   WHAT EACH OUTCOME MEANS.
#
#   PHASE 2 - THE KILL'S CURRENCY. A ladder of unpaced pure-arithmetic bursts,
#   each ~1.5x the last, every one fenced by settle seconds of sleep on both
#   sides. The script is EXPECTED to be killed partway up; where it dies is the
#   measurement. Each surviving rung prints burst=<seconds>, so the boundary is
#   recorded in BOTH currencies at once:
#       kill in SECONDS   = burst= at the largest survivor
#       kill in TICKS     = 2n+2 (loop condition free) or 3n+3 (charged) at the
#                           same n. T15/T16 decide which; the cross-tier ratio
#                           does not care, because the same formula applies at
#                           both tiers.
#   Post-upgrade, exactly one of those two numbers stays put. That is the
#   answer, and it needs no model of the meter at all.
#
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# THE BURST IS THE EXPERIMENT (deliberate, not an oversight)
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
#   Phase 2's bursts violate the "sleep() every 5-8 lines" rule on purpose.
#   Being killed is the reading. Everything OUTSIDE the bursts is paced
#   normally, and every burst function's own FIRST line is its sleep(s), so the
#   loop bookkeeping and the announcement line land on the far side of a sleep
#   from the burst and can never be mistaken for the thing that did the killing.
#   Phase 1 is fully paced and is not supposed to die (see PACING NOTE).
#
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# TIER LOOKUP - read your tier straight off the CALIB line
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
#   CALIB burst8 is one fixed 8-iteration arithmetic burst, identical in every
#   run at every tier: 2 setup lines + 8 iterations of 2 lines = 18 ticks if
#   the while condition is free, 27 if it is charged. So:
#       burst8 ~1.8 - 2.7 s   -> 10 ticks/sec   BASE TIER
#       burst8 ~0.9 - 1.35 s  -> 20 ticks/sec   CPU Mk.II
#       burst8 ~0.45 - 0.68 s -> 40 ticks/sec   CPU Mk.III
#       burst8 ~0.23 - 0.34 s -> 80 ticks/sec   CPU Mk.IV
#   The bands do not overlap, so one number names the tier. This replaces the
#   `neofetch`/`sysinfo` step an earlier draft of this test called for: no such
#   command exists (help_commands.txt and help_processes.txt list the complete
#   set), and server_specs() is remote-only. Self-measurement is the only way.
#   If burst8 prints as a whole number or 0, get_unix_time() is 1-second
#   resolution on this build (T01) - the tier bands are then unusable and only
#   the ladder's rung index survives as a comparison.
#
#   pred_n = round(40 / burst8) is what the DOCUMENTED rule predicts the lethal
#   iteration count must be, at whatever tier this machine turns out to be.
#   The script prints its own falsifiable prediction, in-band, before producing
#   the number that confirms or refutes it. Base tier: expect pred_n 15-22.
#
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# OUTPUT VOCABULARY - every line is a complete datum on its own
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
#   T28 HEAD ... entry_cpu=<c>   Printed off the script's VERY FIRST executable
#       line, above the func defs: a free cross-run carryover probe. ~0 on a
#       cold run (test_cpu_decay saw exactly 0); materially above 0 on a run
#       started seconds after a kill, which would contaminate the card.
#   T28 CONF <text>              Raw contents of ~/.config/blackdoor.conf, or
#       "unreadable". Look for tick_speed=N and check it against the CALIB
#       band. Agreement validates nu.local_tick_rate() for every future run;
#       disagreement means menu.bd has been printing a fiction.
#   FLOOR A j=<j> cpu=<c>        4 samples, 1/sec, lean loop.
#   FLOOR B j=<j> cpu=<c>        4 samples, 1/sec, +3 executed lines per sec.
#   T28 CARD1 floorA= floorB= slope=     the meter half of the card, printed
#       BEFORE the ladder starts, so a ladder death still leaves phase 1 whole.
#   T28 CALIB burst8=<s>s pred_n=<n>     tier + the script's own prediction.
#   RUNG r=<r> n=<k> pre=<c>     printed BEFORE the burst. If this is the last
#       line in the paste, burst size k killed the script. Complete on its own.
#       pre= proves, per rung, that the rung started from the floor rather than
#       assuming the settle was enough.
#   OK r=<r> n=<k> burst=<s>s t=+<T>s    printed only AFTER the post-burst
#       sleep, so it proves the burst survived. burst= is the meter-free ruler.
#       t= is the global timeline: consecutive t= differences minus the fixed
#       3*settle seconds per rung recover burst= a second, independent way.
#   T28 DONE rungs_done=<R> next_n=<k>   nothing died. Re-run with rungs=R+3.
#
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# PRE-REGISTERED PREDICTIONS - written before any run
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
#   B1 BASELINE, meter:  floorA = 10.0 (matching test_cpu_decay exactly).
#   B2 BASELINE, slope:  slope = 30.0 if one line is worth 10 meter points and
#      the floor is pure density; slope = 0 if the floor is a constant that
#      ignores density altogether (which the steady 10.0 at 4-6 lines/sec
#      already hints at, and which would be a finding in its own right).
#   B3 BASELINE, ladder: dies at n = 12, 18 or 27 (26-84 ticks, 2.6-8.4s at
#      base tier), bracketing the documented 5-second rule, and within +/-1
#      rung of the CALIB line's own pred_n.
#   B4 BASELINE, repeat: both baseline runs die at the SAME rung.
#   U1 POST-UPGRADE, tier-normalised meter: floorA and slope BOTH divide by the
#      tier multiplier (10.0 -> 5.0 and 30.0 -> 15.0 on Mk.II).
#   U2 POST-UPGRADE, absolute-tick meter: floorA and slope BOTH unchanged.
#   U3 POST-UPGRADE, wall-clock kill: n* multiplies by the tier multiplier
#      (dies at 18 now -> dies at 140-210 on Mk.IV) and burst= at the largest
#      survivor is UNCHANGED in seconds.
#   U4 POST-UPGRADE, fixed-tick kill: n* is UNCHANGED and burst= at the largest
#      survivor DIVIDES by the tier multiplier.
#
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# WHAT EACH OUTCOME MEANS
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
#   CONFIRMS "tier-normalised meter" (U1): field_notes' CPU LOAD claim and
#       utils/net.bd's local_tick_rate() comment are both upheld. Throttle
#       thresholds are tier-portable; a faster CPU really does clear
#       throttle()'s while loop in fewer retries with zero code changes.
#   REFUTES it (U2): the meter is an absolute tick counter. Every percentage in
#       field_notes is a statement about ONE tier, the REFRAMING block's
#       "max_cpu_usage_limit that stays equally high regardless of tier" is the
#       correct account, and the guidance in net.bd lines 70-78 must be struck.
#   FLOOR MOVES BUT SLOPE DOES NOT (or the reverse): the reading is
#       unit*lines + ambient with the two terms scaling differently. Report
#       both numbers separately and do NOT collapse them into one "meter unit".
#       The slope is the unit; the floor is unit + ambient.
#   CONFIRMS "wall-clock kill" (U3): the documented 5-second rule is literally
#       implemented. A CPU upgrade multiplies safe burst length by the tier,
#       "pace every 5-8 lines" relaxes proportionally, and the upgrade is worth
#       buying for headroom alone.
#   REFUTES it (U4): the kill is a ~50-TICK rule wearing a seconds costume. A
#       CPU upgrade buys literally zero burst headroom - it makes the same
#       amount of work finish sooner but the same unpaced burst still kills.
#       Every pacing rule in this codebase is then permanently tier-independent
#       and must stay written in LINE COUNTS, never in seconds.
#   NEITHER INVARIANT HOLDS post-upgrade: the trigger is integrating something
#       that is neither seconds nor ticks (a meter-level integral, say). That
#       is T13's territory - hand it over with both numbers.
#   BASELINE LADDER SURVIVES TO THE TOP RUNG: either this machine is already on
#       a high tier (check CALIB first - it will say so) or the unpaced-burst
#       lethality model is wrong. Re-run with rungs=12 and report immediately.
#   BASELINE LADDER DIES AT n=8 (the first rung, 18-27 ticks, 1.8-2.7s at base
#       tier): the kill fires far sooner than 5 seconds of saturation and the
#       documented rule is simply wrong. Re-run with start=4.
#   THE TWO BASELINE RUNS DIE AT DIFFERENT RUNGS: STOP. That is a variable
#       lethal threshold at a FIXED tier, which is a sufficient mechanism for
#       the entire +40.7 / +111.0 / +40.3 / +65.5 / +64.3s spread this whole
#       investigation is chasing. It is T11's question, not this one - run a
#       third repeat, report all three, and hand the result to T11.
#   PHASE 1 KILLS THE SCRIPT: a fully sleep(1)-paced loop of 9 lines per second
#       is lethal. That would overturn the project's central pacing rule and
#       supersedes everything else on this card. Report the FLOOR lines as-is.
#
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# HOW TO RUN - get_param binds POSITIONALLY, no -flag= syntax
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
#   ps            confirm nothing else is running (the meter is an AGGREGATE
#                 across all your processes - a stray job makes the card junk)
#   jobs          same
#   browse 45.77.12.8/hardware.html      the shop page. Which CPU upgrade is
#                 offered next tells you which ones you already own. This is
#                 the only external tier check that is known to exist; paste it.
#   run T28              defaults: start=8 rungs=9 settle=3 reps=4
#   run T28 8 12 3 4     to extend the ladder without EDITING
#                 THE FILE. Never change the ladder between tiers by hand - the
#                 rung sizes are generated by a formula (k -> k*3/2, from 8)
#                 precisely so that the before and after runs are
#                 byte-identical. With default start=8 that yields
#                 8, 12, 18, 27, 40, 60, 90, 135, 202. It does not matter
#                 whether `/` truncates or returns a float on this build: the
#                 ladder is deterministic and identical at every tier either
#                 way, which is the only property the comparison needs.
#
#   REPEATS: 2 runs now (baseline card), and 2 runs immediately after any CPU
#   purchase, BEFORE buying anything else - buy the CPU alone, so the RAM slot
#   count and everything else is held constant across the comparison.
#   If the two baseline runs disagree on the rung, run a third and see above.
#
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# PACING NOTE (every line costs 1 tick; unpaced bursts kill scripts)
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
#   Outside the phase-2 bursts, no stretch between two sleeps exceeds 5 lines.
#   Phase 1 arm B executes ~9 lines per second, which is the highest sustained
#   density anywhere in this script. That is deliberate (it is the slope) and it
#   is bounded: reps defaults to 4, so even if arm B saturates the meter
#   completely it cannot satisfy a 5-CONSECUTIVE-SECOND rule. Arm A runs first,
#   so an arm-B death still leaves arm A's four FLOOR lines on screen.
#
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# OBSERVER EFFECT
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
#   The bursts contain ZERO log() and ZERO get_cpu() - pure arithmetic - so the
#   thing being measured is uncontaminated by the thing measuring it. All
#   instrumentation sits outside the burst behind sleep(settle) on both sides.
#   get_cpu() appears exactly three places: the entry probe, phase 1 (where it
#   is the observable, and where both arms pay for it identically so it cancels
#   out of the slope), and the per-rung pre= reading (outside the window, ahead
#   of a sleep). The measuring lines are identical at every rung and at every
#   tier, so nothing about the instrument changes across the comparison this
#   card exists to support.
#   burst= depends on get_unix_time() being a real clock (T01) and on its two
#   bracketing reads being free (T05/T11). If they are not free, they add the
#   same constant at both tiers and cancel out of every ratio here anyway.
#
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# KNOWN CONFOUNDS
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
#   LADDER CARRYOVER. Several bursts live in one run. If load does not fully
#   clear across settle seconds, a late rung starts hot and dies for the wrong
#   reason. pre= on every RUNG line is the check: if pre= climbs rung over
#   rung, re-run with settle=8 and say so. Whether a sleep resets the kill
#   timer AT ALL is T12's question and T12 has not reported - until it does,
#   every n* here is a LOWER bound on the true fresh-run boundary.
#   RUNG RESOLUTION. The ladder steps ~1.5x, so a rung index pins n* only to
#   +/-50%. Mk.II's predicted 2x move is only ~1.7 rungs - too close to call on
#   the index alone. That is exactly why burst= in seconds is the primary
#   discriminator: it is continuous, and the two hypotheses predict a clean 2x
#   split in it even on the smallest upgrade.

var c_entry = get_cpu()

func floor_arm(reps, extra, tag):
    sleep(2)
    var j = 0
    var x = 0
    var c = 0
    while j < reps:
        sleep(1)
        c = get_cpu()
        if extra > 0:
            x = 1
            x = 2
            x = 3
        log("FLOOR " + tag + " j=" + str(j) + " cpu=" + str(c))
        j = j + 1
    return c

sleep(1)

func burst(n, s):
    sleep(s)
    var t = get_unix_time()
    var j = 0
    var x = 0
    while j < n:
        x = j * 2
        j = j + 1
    return get_unix_time() - t

sleep(1)

var T0 = get_unix_time()
var START = int(get_param("start", "8").replace("\"", "").trim())
var RUNGS = int(get_param("rungs", "9").replace("\"", "").trim())
sleep(1)
var SET = int(get_param("settle", "3").replace("\"", "").trim())
var REPS = int(get_param("reps", "4").replace("\"", "").trim())
sleep(1)
var conf = "unreadable"
try:
    conf = read_local("~/.config/blackdoor.conf")
catch e:
    conf = "unreadable"
sleep(1)
log("T28 HEAD start=" + str(START) + " rungs=" + str(RUNGS) + " settle=" + str(SET) + " reps=" + str(REPS) + " entry_cpu=" + str(c_entry))
sleep(1)
log("T28 CONF " + conf)
sleep(1)

var fa = floor_arm(REPS, 0, "A")
sleep(2)
var fb = floor_arm(REPS, 3, "B")
sleep(2)
var slope = fb - fa
log("T28 CARD1 floorA=" + str(fa) + " floorB=" + str(fb) + " slope=" + str(slope))
sleep(2)

var cal = burst(8, SET)
sleep(SET)
var pred = 0
if cal > 0:
    pred = round(40 / cal)
log("T28 CALIB burst8=" + str(cal) + "s pred_n=" + str(pred) + " (0 = clock too coarse)")
sleep(2)
log("T28 PHASE2 ladder begins - this script is SUPPOSED to be killed")
sleep(2)

var k = START
var r = 0
var el = 0
var pre = 0
while r < RUNGS:
    sleep(SET)
    pre = get_cpu()
    log("RUNG r=" + str(r) + " n=" + str(k) + " pre=" + str(pre))
    el = burst(k, SET)
    sleep(SET)
    log("OK r=" + str(r) + " n=" + str(k) + " burst=" + str(el) + "s t=+" + str(get_unix_time() - T0) + "s")
    k = int(round(k * 3 / 2))
    r = r + 1

log("T28 DONE rungs_done=" + str(RUNGS) + " next_n=" + str(k) + " nothing died - re-run with rungs=" + str(RUNGS + 3))
